# core.autocrlf=true on a Windows checkout rewrites LF to CRLF, and a shell
# script with CRLF line endings does not run: sh reads the trailing \r as part
# of the command and fails on the first line. deploy/migrate.sh is the one that
# matters -- it is how migrations reach the Render database from a workstation,
# and it would break on a fresh clone rather than in the working copy it was
# written in.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The same hazard, and it bites harder here. deploy/migrate.sh records a
# sha256 of each migration FILE in schema_migrations, and compares it on every
# later run -- so the checksum depends on the bytes on disk, line endings
# included. A migration applied from an LF checkout and re-read from a CRLF one
# hashes differently, and the runner correctly refuses to continue: it cannot
# tell "someone edited an applied migration" from "someone checked it out on
# Windows". Production applied 002 as afc82e95..., this checkout hashed it as
# 0211d93b..., and not one byte of SQL differed between them.
#
# Pinning LF makes the recorded checksum a property of the migration rather
# than of the machine that happened to apply it.
*.sql text eol=lf
